the word disabled athlete I absolutely  hate it I don't look at myself as  disabled at all I have a disability  everybody in this world has a disability  of some sort but you're only disabled if  you believe you are I'm just an athlete  that needs a couple extra tools this  great big story was made possible by  Geico believe it  Geico could save you 15% or more on car  insurance my competitive history started  out on a BMX bike when I was like 13  years old which quickly evolved into  motocross and then got into racing  snowmobiles at the peak of my pro career  I had finished fourth place in the  national championship series I'd landed  on the podium a handful of times in 2008  during a qualifying race in northern  Michigan I was thrown from my machine  which caused a compound fracture that  severed the main artery that feeds my  lower leg it was apparent three days  later that amputation was the only way  to basically save my life I was floored  I mean I didn't see it coming at all my  profession and livelihood is to be  racing a snowmobile or a dirt bike you  know initially I'm like I'm gonna be  done racing I'm gonna be done competing  but about five weeks after the injury  had happened I got to try out my new  prosthetic leg the first leg that I got  it worked okay for walking it was really  basic mechanical leg actually got on my  snowmobile within a week or so of  getting it and right away I understood  that yeah this leg is definitely not  gonna be the ticket for me to get back  into the sports that I love I was  presented with this problem basically I  needed to absorb impact with my leg like  my quadricep muscles do I know  suspension components from the dirt  bikes and snowmobiles so I'm looking at  this as a suspension component I've  needed shock a spring and a frame to  hold it all and make it move in the  directions I needed to move  and I drew on it for about five weeks or  so just pencil sketches and pages and  pages of designs and then from there I  went to the shop and started cutting  part I had all the pieces and assemble  it put it on my leg and then immediately  went over to my dirt bike and went for a  ride seven months after my accident I  was racing dirt bikes on a leg that I  built in my garage the original moto  knee was developed for me to get back on  my dirt bike and that summer after  meeting a whole bunch of other amputees  I realized that there's so many other  people that can utilize the equipment I  was working on so I started thinking  about creating a company around it the  coolest part about what I'm doing is it  allows me to continue to be an athlete  so I do all the testing on the racetrack  and the practice track which gets me  into a lot of new stuff as well like  snowboarding it turns out I got pretty  good at it I enjoyed it got talked into  competing for the first time and that  evolved into traveling around the world  on the World Cup tour and earning my  spot on the 2018 Paralympic snowboarder  team and won a gold medal and border  cross and a silver medal and bang slalom  and I'm a nine-time X Games gold  medalist between adaptive racing and  dirt bike snowmobiles and now snow bikes  you know I've kind of reached my goals  with all that so this year I'm gonna  step it up a notch and I'm gonna try and  qualify into the pro snow bike race I  tell you what it really sucks to be an  amputee sometimes but there's so many  different amazing things that I've been  able to experience since my injury  happen I'm making the most of the  situation and I'm still living life to  the fullest  [Music]
 

